  "explain": [
    {
      "t": "intro",
      "es": "En presente usamos <b>do / does</b> para negar y preguntar. En pasado, el ayudante es <mark>DID</mark>, ¡y es el mismo para todas las personas! <b>Did</b> ya lleva el pasado dentro, así que el verbo principal vuelve a su <b>forma base</b> (el infinitivo sin to): <i>I went</i> → I <b>didn't go</b> → <b>Did</b> you <b>go</b>?",
      "en": "In the past, the helper is <b>did</b>. It's the same for everyone and it carries the past, so the main verb goes back to its <b>base form</b>.",
      "formula": [
        "Sujeto + *didn't* + verbo base",
        "*Did* + sujeto + verbo base + …?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "t": "rule",
      "title": "1 · Negativa",
      "title_en": "Negative",
      "icon": "⛔",
      "color": "red",
      "es": "Para negar en pasado ponemos <b>didn't</b> (did not) delante del verbo, y el verbo vuelve a su <b>forma base</b>: played → <b>didn't play</b>, went → <b>didn't go</b>. Vale igual para verbos regulares e irregulares.",
      "en": "<b>didn't</b> + base verb. Regular or irregular, it works the same.",
      "formula": [
        "I / you / he / she / we / they + *didn't* + play / go"
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "en": "I <b>didn't watch</b> TV yesterday.",
          "es": "Ayer no vi la tele.",
          "icon": "📺"
        },
        {
          "en": "Rocky <b>didn't eat</b> his dinner.",
          "es": "Rocky no se comió la cena.",
          "icon": "🐕"
        },
        {
          "en": "We <b>didn't go</b> to the beach.",
          "es": "No fuimos a la playa.",
          "icon": "🏖️"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
